-- title:
--   Inertia fixes the prime-to-p toric points
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $N_0 \ne 0$ and a prime $p$ with $p \nmid N_0$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $A.\mathrm{LiesOverPrime}\,p$, i.e. $p$ lies in the set of non-units of $A$, and a level datum $\Lambda$ of type `LevelData N₀ p A`: an $A$-point $\sigma_A$ of the base whose composition with the point `barPt A` is the generic point, a scheme $X$ over the base carrying a relative group law, together with bijections from $J_0(N_0)$ onto the points over the generic point and from the analogous group over the residue field of $A$ onto the points over the residual section. Assume moreover $\Lambda.\mathrm{IsJacobian}$ (abelian-scheme property bundle, commutativity of the group law, additivity and Galois-equivariance of `pts`, additivity of `ptsSp`, agreement of reduction of points mod $\ell$, and realisation of the Hecke algebra by endomorphisms over the base). Let $O$ be a Néron object of type `JZeroNeronObjectAtP N₀ p hpN₀ A hA Λ`, i.e. a smooth, separated, quasi-compact, surjective group scheme over the base with locally finite type, preconnected fibres, multiplication by $n>0$ flat and surjective, proper generic fibre, a toric rank and associated toric data, and a bijection `pts` from $J_0(N_0p)$ onto the points over the generic point which is additive, Galois-equivariant and Hecke-equivariant. Let $m$ be coprime to $p$. Then every $\sigma$ in the image in $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ of the inertia subgroup of $A$ (inside its decomposition subgroup) fixes every element $y$ of $O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,m$, the subgroup of $J_0(N_0p)$ generated by the range of $O.\mathrm{toricPoint}\,m$ (and trivial if $m = 0$): $\sigma \cdot y = y$.
--
--   This records that the prime-to-$p$ part of the toric torsion of the level-$N_0p$ Néron object consists of inertia-invariant points, reflecting the fact that $\mu_m$ is étale over $A$ when $p \nmid m$. It feeds the comparison of the toric points with the monodromy toric part and, through it, the analysis of the inertia action on $J_0(N_0p)$ used in level lowering at $p$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_JZeroNeronObjectAtP_smul_eq_self_of_mem_inertiaSubgroupIn_of_mem_toricPts.lean

